   129	### C10. Frames and ABI
   130	
   131	**Freeze:** logical memory footprints and the frame law (an operation preserves disjoint memory outside its permitted footprint); call-frame ownership/restoration; ABI signatures relating typed arguments/results to registers, stack slots and preserved machine state. Include frame size/alignment, return convention, clobbers, stack-accounting handoff, extern validity/retention and launcher entry/exit relations.
   132	
   133	**Rule:** internal calls use an explicit target convention proved compatible with these contracts. At Memory CFG, lower the hybrid Core representation to explicit byte/provenance operations and ABI calls, proving its simulation against C4’s projection. BIR carries the complete resulting contract. Frame accounting binds to the final artifact, including spills, cleanup and launcher/runtime contributions, not merely source locals. Stack facts are separate from heap accounting. Foreign retention needs the decided `kept`/`Kept<H>` contract; FFI-safe types and no-enum restriction remain. Logical frame theorems do not add a source frame clause or snapshot construct.
   134	
   135	**Authority:** Core Q3.12, Q8.5–Q8.6, [pipeline](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-pipeline-2026-11-06), [contracts/P18](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-contracts), [stack bounds](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-07-stack-randomness). **Later:** ABI implementations, extern syntax at 25, stronger program frame proofs. Neither the frame interface nor packed access waits until then.
   136	
   137	## 3. Pipeline and validation contract
   138	
   139	Retain the decided stages:
   140	
   141	`bytes → lexer → LL(1) parser → AST → resolve → HIR → elaboration → Core → checker → CheckedCore → Mono Core → Place MIR → Memory CFG → SSA → BIR`.
   142	
   143	BIR feeds the LLVM printer or the proved BIR→LCB adapter. **Memory CFG stays the single layout/ABI/provenance lowering boundary**; retract P2's folding proposal. Earlier semantics uses C4’s hybrid state and read-only layout interface; representation simulations relate it to this explicit lowering boundary.
   144	
   145	**Frontend:** retract P1's parse/print criterion. Define byte/token/grammar acceptance independently of the lexer/parser implementation, and source binding, typing and source-to-Core correspondence independently of resolve/elaboration. Prove lexer/parser correspondence to the independent grammar, resolution's binding preservation and elaboration's preservation of meaning, sequencing, cleanup, identities and source sites. Prove checker equivalence to independent admission judgments. Parse/print tests can supplement these proofs but cannot replace them. This retains Core Q8.3/Q9.13 and the decided direct frontend proofs.
   146	
   147	Keep direct proofs for sequencing, cleanup, representation and the BIR→LCB adapter. Preserve the decided option of **proved validators for Mono and SSA**, as well as SSA optimizations, register allocation and linking. A validator's acceptance theorem establishes the required correspondence; running a checker without that theorem is not proof. Mono re-admits its output.
   148	
   149	**Certificates and maximal behavior:** retain the [compiler-execution decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): every authoritative compilation emits kernel-checked certificates binding the exact source bytes through independent judgments/validated edges to the exact emitted BIR, and binding the actual backend input to it. A digest establishes identity, not semantic correspondence. Native execution of proved Lean definitions is not itself proof; any gap retains explicit compiler/runtime/toolchain assumptions. After slice 0 use the decided per-edge certificates.
   150	
   151	Each lowering proves refinement of maximal behaviors, including termination, abort/trap, blocking and infinite execution with their traces under matched allowed choices. Target steps may stutter finitely; a progress or well-founded argument excludes infinite silent target stuttering that replaces source progress or completion. Also relate each finite target prefix to compatible source prefixes; their limits must satisfy C8’s infinite-behavior adequacy, not use unrelated witnesses. The default method is forward simulation with finite stuttering and target determinism under fixed admissible choices, yielding backward refinement once receptiveness, progress, initial/final correspondence and the required determinacy hypotheses are proved. Where choices cannot be fixed compatibly or these hypotheses fail, prove backward simulation directly (or an equivalent behavior-inclusion theorem). Do not assume all source programs terminate to justify stuttering. See [A1, “Fuel and divergence” and “Behaviour refinement”](../legacy/docs/compiler/A1_BOUNDARIES.md).
   152	
   153	**Native closure (P3):** retain Q8.1’s proved source→BIR, qualified native and conditional image claim, LP0 after slice 1 and R-0505’s later final-image closure. Question 8 separately asks whether to advance it to 0.1. Any validator must bind exact linked image bytes, encodings, relocations, symbols, entry state, launcher/ABI, retained dependencies and target to LCB instruction semantics. Hardware/ISA correspondence, loader/OS/environment, foreign/runtime code, adequate stack and timing/wipe premises remain named unless discharged. LLVM stays unverified.
   154	
   155	**Qualification (P4):** run LLVM, LCB and the hybrid Core Lean evaluator on **every supported example and controlled test**, with the same input bytes, target/layout profile, grant configuration and compatible scripted external responses. Frontend certificates bind this Core program to the source; representation simulations relate its raw-byte/layout observations to both native paths. For each native run, check that its complete trace is an allowed observation of a Core execution under those inputs: this is a refinement check. Exposed stack addresses and initialized padding can differ between correct LLVM and LCB runs; find and validate a separate admissible Core choice witness for each, without normalizing away raw observations. Native execution cannot be forced to follow the evaluator’s oracle. Require exact equality where behavior is deterministic. Failed witness search is inconclusive unless exclusion is proved. Compare complete observations: ordered requests/responses/output, terminal classification, trap kind/site/line and observable final external state. Use bounded scripted environments for effectful/blocking cases. Report unsupported or inconclusive runs explicitly; do not count `outOfFuel` as agreement or divergence. Compare leakage separately where the claimed backend evidence supports it. Never require arbitrary user programs to execute three times. Tests qualify implementations; the LCB path is **proved under named premises**, and LLVM remains unverified. Keep the two premise sets separate.
   156	
   157	## 4. Revision register
   158	
   159	No revision is enacted here. Each question in section 5 has its own answer; an umbrella approval cannot choose these independently.
   160	
   161	| Rows | Scope and status | Question |
   162	|---|---|---|
   163	| OD126; Q3.7/Q3.9/Q3.10 | Generalize address/profile interface, preserving provenance rules and zero-byte exception; existing 64-bit little-endian profile unchanged, including Q3.10’s `base + size ≤ 2^64−1` one-past bound. Any generalized profile needs an analogous checked bound. | 3 |
   164	| Q3.5; Q1.11/Q2.4/Q4.6; TYPE-19; FFI-7 | Q3.5 fixes u64 lengths/indices across profiles. Q1.11 rejects counts above u64 as TooLarge; Q2.4 fixes Drained.at: u64; Q4.6 byte offsets/lengths use that index contract. Target-sized usize would revise these and layout-query results. Recommendation retains these semantics and permits only proved narrower representations. | 7 |
   165	| OD126; FFI-7 | Native enum tag/payload layout changes existing i32-tag profile separately from target abstraction. | 4 |
   166	| Q1.7 | Proved release-context erasure, including owning Text where applicable; no unconditional one-pointer claim. | 6 |
   167	| Q7.1/Q7.10; Q9.7 | Lawful registry instances rather than a permanently closed inventory; preserve existing grant contracts. Entropy details deferred. | 16, 14 |
   168	| FFI-7/10 | Natural native struct layout already supersedes unspecified-layout prose under runtime decision; retain repr restrictions. | Retained clarification |
   169	| Q8.1; early-evidence decision; R-0505 | Keep current conditional endpoint and later closure; advancing it requires separate approval. | 8 |
   170	
   171	Q9.11/OD144 already decide post-cleanup ensures timing. The SPARK decision already decides source struct invariants. Neither is a new rule. C4’s hybrid/representation completion is a proposed formalization choice, not authority to revise raw access. Exact trap constructors and byte columns are not inferred from Q6.7 or ArithmeticError. OD106/ARITH-10 already fix conversion errors; Q2.3 and Q3.8 already fix destruction orders. Questions 12 and 13 are removed as fully decided; later question numbers are retained for review tracking.
   172	
   173	## 5. Separately answerable owner questions
   174	
   175	Each recommendation is a proposal, not an implemented language decision. “Defer” preserves existing decided rules and leaves detailed instances to the named slice.
   176	
   177	**Q1. Foundation scope.** Recommend approving the interface/law boundary of C1–C10, with freeze only after implementation, kernel proofs, interaction tests, independent review and owner acceptance. Runner-up: freeze smaller integrated scopes incrementally. Neither approves the other numbered questions.
   178	**Q2. Hybrid state and representation completion.** Recommend C4’s structured-place/byte-block state, coherent projection, pointer fragments and explicit initialization/padding laws. Runner-up: use byte blocks for all Core storage. The hybrid keeps typed proofs small while raw operations remain executable; both must preserve Q3.7 and raw observations.
   179	**Q3. Target abstraction.** Recommend read-only profiles separating address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; generalize Q3.7/Q3.9/Q3.10 without changing existing profile constants or reconstruction policy. Runner-up: separate semantics per profile under OD126. No usize or enum change is included.
   180	**Q4. Enum layout.** Recommend retaining the existing profile’s i32 tags for now, deferring a compact u8/u16/u32 algorithm to the aggregate/layout slice and a separate owner answer. Runner-up: approve now the smallest tag fitting the variant count (u8 ≤256, u16 ≤65,536, u32 ≤2^32), declaration-order tags, aligned common payload and rounded aggregate size, rejecting larger counts. This changes OD126/FFI-7 independently of question 3.
   181	**Q5. Niches.** Three options: (a) exactly one null-empty/non-null single-pointer-payload niche, with pointer-size, provenance, ownership and release-context proofs; (b) tagged layout now, future niches only by owner decision; (c) no niches permanently. Recommend **(b)**: the foundation needs representation laws, not a permanent optimization inventory. Runner-up **(a)** gives compact optional ownership but adds a special eligibility proof; multiword release contexts and nullable raw pointers are ineligible. Round-2 reviewers split: Codex and Kimi prefer (b); agy prefers (c). (c) is uniform but unnecessarily permanent.
   182	**Q6. Release-context erasure.** Recommend retaining sufficient stable context and erasing only with a release/identity/dependency preservation proof and no per-instance state. Runner-up: always store context. This separately revises Q1.7; it does not fix Text’s internal word count.
   183	**Q7. usize.** Three options: (a) target-sized usize avoids 64-bit lengths/storage/arithmetic on 8/16/32-bit chips, but changes the cross-profile meaning deliberately fixed by Q3.5 and the dependent contracts in section 4; (b) retain u64 semantics everywhere, with explicit checked target limits, at a cost on small targets; (c) retain those u64 semantics and prove a narrower internal representation wherever bounds justify it, using widening or fallback when they do not. Recommend **(c)**, following [PHILOSOPHY](../docs/PHILOSOPHY.md)’s explicit, checkable semantics and Q3.5. This cannot promise all lengths fit a machine word: zero-sized collections can have large logical counts. Representation narrowing needs a simulation preserving arithmetic, failures and observations, not silent truncation or new target-dependent overflow. Codex and agy favor (a); Kimi favors (b). A new source usize remains a separately approved revision; no isize is implied.
   184	**Q8. Native-image milestone.** Recommend keeping R-0505’s later closure and Q8.1’s conditional endpoint. Runner-up: require proved exact AArch64 linked-image validation by 0.1, explicitly revising Q8.1, early-evidence and proof-infrastructure milestones while retaining all undischarged premises. Qualification is question 17, not bundled here.
   185	**Q9. Trap inventory and spellings.** Recommend deferring concrete constructors/renderings until trap semantics needs them, before their first supported slice. Runner-up: approve `arithmetic(ArithmeticError) | bounds | invalidRange | abort`, rendered `overflow`, `division_by_zero`, `invalid_shift`, `bounds`, `invalid_range`, `abort`, with one trailing newline. ArithmeticError’s decided cases do not decide this wrapper or its strings. Preserve the runtime line format and exits either way. Codex and Kimi support deferral before first use; agy wants the inventory and spellings approved now to avoid churn.
   186	**Q10. Columns and tabs.** Recommend byte columns, tabs counting one byte, with conformance tests; runner-up: Unicode-scalar columns, tabs counting one scalar. The runtime decision fixes only 1-based columns, so neither convention is already decided.
   187	**Q11. UTF-8 failure classification.** Recommend deferring mapping to the text slice’s operation contracts while preserving UTF-8 validity and no hidden traps. Runner-up: map unchecked UTF-8 boundary failures to proposed invalidRange. This does not turn handled checked errors into traps.
   188	**Q14. Entropy shape.** Recommend deferring exact launch-grant type, operations and ending contract to slice 6, consistent with Q7.10 and the randomness schedule. Runner-up: approve a nullary Entropy grant only together with a complete instance contract. No nullary shape is assumed by C5.
   189	**Q15. Struct-invariant implementation.** Recommend deferring the source feature to slice 17, while writing the decided SPARK exposure definition in the spec before freeze and retaining Q9.11/OD144 post-cleanup ensures semantics. Runner-up: implement the source feature earlier if an interaction proof needs it. There is no new timing choice to approve.
   190	**Q16. Registry extensibility.** Recommend lawful typed extensions preserving existing primitive/grant entries and exhaustive coverage of every selected instance; unknown operations are refused. Runner-up: retain closed Q9.7/Q7.10 inventories and seek an owner amendment for each addition. Schema compatibility must preserve old records; no new source spelling is implied.
   191	**Q17. Differential qualification.** Recommend P4’s per-native-trace refinement check against allowed Core observations on every supported controlled example/test, with exact equality for deterministic behavior. Keep exposed addresses and padding as real observations; allow separate validated Core choice witnesses. Runner-up: staged coverage until witness validation is executable, explicitly reporting unsupported/inconclusive cases. Codex’s round-2 objection to mandatory equality is accepted; Kimi and agy’s agreement with draft 2 does not solve native choice matching. Preserve the decided two-backend comparison; testing never replaces proof.

