1125:| OD10 | <a id="od10"></a>Arithmetic: checked by default, named wrapping and saturating operations, shifts with a checked count; an abort runs no deferred call | T-ARITH, recorded as settled (today's behaviour), with §4.5's abort rule | exits 2h and 2j, so V1a, V1b and V1c (checked `+ - *` and their traps); R-0493 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: integer arithmetic is checked by default; wrapping and saturating arithmetic are named operations; the shift count is checked; an abort runs no deferred call **Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): plain arithmetic is admitted only with evidence that it cannot fail; wrapping, saturating and checked (`Result`) forms cover the rest; overflow is still never silent.** |
1194:| OD79 | <a id="od79"></a>`assert` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the proof-annotation statement `assert expr;`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Add `assert`. Extended 2026-11-06 by `assert(e) by thm(...)` ([SPARK row](#decided-project-spark-2026-11-06)) |
1195:| OD80 | <a id="od80"></a>`assume` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the proof-annotation statement `assume expr;`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep `assume`, tracked: every claim depending on it is marked assumed **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): `assume` cannot admit an operation in safe code.** |
1197:| OD82 | <a id="od82"></a>Loop contract attributes and where attributes attach (added 2026-10-30, item C5 (R7) of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains `#[invariant(e)]` and `#[variant(e)]` before a loop, and whether attributes attach by kind (declaration attributes to the next declaration, loop attributes to the next loop). Options: (a) add them to the grammar and attach attributes by kind; (b) remove them from the parser; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a): attach by kind, declaration attributes to the next declaration, `#[invariant]` and `#[variant]` to the next loop. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts them | CON-2, STMT-12, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Add; attributes attach by kind **Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): `#[variant]` also attaches to a function, one measure per recursive cycle; `#[may_not_terminate("reason")]` also attaches as a prefix to an expression (an indirect call), through one attribute-prefix production in statements.** |
1212:| OD97 | <a id="od97"></a>A build profile that omits or demands an overflow check (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a build profile may omit the run-time check of an integer operation that no proof discharges, or refuse such an operation at compile time. Bears on ARITH-3, ARITH-9. The current compiler: every profile emits every check, and no option turns one off; ARITH-3's rationale says a profile changes reports, never code generation, and OD10 decided that arithmetic is checked "by default". The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §3.1 instead let a release profile omit a check that is "explicitly configured off and reported", and a provable or high-integrity profile refuse an unproved overflow site. Options: (a) every profile keeps every check that no discharged proof removes; (b) a release profile may turn checks off when configured and reported, never wrapping; (c) as (a), and a profile may also refuse an operation whose no-overflow obligation is not discharged. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-97 | see OPEN-97 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep every check; a profile may refuse undischarged ops **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): every build refuses an operation without discharged evidence.** |
1216:| OD101 | <a id="od101"></a>What a trap writes (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing ARITHMETIC_POLICY and VALUE_MODEL). Question: Whether a run-time trap writes a message naming the operation, its operands and its source location before the process ends, and in what form. Bears on STMT-2, STMT-7, ARITH-3. The current compiler: the binary writes nothing and ends by `SIGABRT`, and `--interp` reports a run-time error naming the trap (`arithmetic overflow (checked +)`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §9.2 described a message `concrete: arithmetic overflow in function 'f' at file:line` with the operation, the operands and the policy. Options: (a) the binary writes nothing; (b) it writes one line naming the trap and its location; (c) the §9.2 form. Austral: not checked. Raised by the absorption of ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md (2026-10-30). | see OPEN-101 | see OPEN-101 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): One line naming the trap and location. Its form, decided 2026-11-06: [runtime row](#decided-project-runtime-2026-11-06) |
1233:| OD118 | <a id="od118"></a>A proven run-time violation in trusted code (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether an index the compiler proves out of bounds, or a division it proves to be by zero, is refused in `trusted` and `with(Unsafe)` code as in safe code. Bears on MEM-6, ARITH-3, TRUST-5, TRUST-12. The current compiler: `a[5]` on a `[Int; 3]`, or `x / 0`, is refused in safe code (E0900, `error[runtime-safety]`) and compiles in a `trusted fn` (the index also in a function declaring `Unsafe`), where it aborts at run time on both consumers. MEM-6 states the refusal with no exemption; [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) C7 records the exemption as intended. Options: (a) refuse everywhere, as MEM-6 reads; (b) keep the exemption and state it in MEM-6. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-118 | see OPEN-118 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse everywhere **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): refusal of proven violations becomes refusal of unproven safety, everywhere.** |
1235:| OD120 | <a id="od120"></a>A vacuous contract (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a contract whose precondition is unsatisfiable is refused by the language or only reported, under a project policy. Bears on CON-1, CON-2. The current compiler: `#[requires(x > 5 && x < 3)]` compiles; a decided-unsatisfiable precondition makes the claim `vacuous`, never `proved`, and E0613 refuses it only under the `forbid-vacuous` policy (`Concrete.Policy`), not in a plain build. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) states the reporting rule. Options: (a) a language refusal in every build; (b) tooling only: always reported `vacuous`, refused under a policy, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-120 | see OPEN-120 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Tooling: reported `vacuous`, refused under policy **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a vacuous precondition used for safety admission is refused in every build.** |
1240:| OD125 | <a id="od125"></a>Contracts at run time (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[requires]`, `#[ensures]` or loop `#[invariant]` that no proof discharges is checked at run time, trapping when it fails, or is only a claim. Bears on CON-1, CON-2, STMT-2. The current compiler: contracts are erased metadata: a call that violates `#[requires(x > 0)]` runs normally, in the binary and in `--interp`, and `assert` elaborates to proof evidence only (syntax chapter introduction). [ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md) lists contract-violation traps among the fatal failures. Options: (a) claims only, never checked at run time, as in the current compiler; (b) checked at run time and trapping unless discharged; (c) checked under a build profile only (OPEN-97, OPEN-124). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-125 | see OPEN-125 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Claims only **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a claim used for safety admission needs closed evidence.** |

