{"id": "Q3.8", "question": "How do zero-sized elements behave?", "options": "logical slots with independent ownership / forbid them / identify slots by address", "reason": "Length and element obligations remain real even when byte stride is zero; equal addresses never justify duplication, overlapping ownership or skipped destruction.", "rec": "Zero-sized slots own their obligations; they occupy no bytes and may share addresses", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F13. Each zero-sized element still has its own obligation and is destroyed in index order. Ownership and identity never come from address equality.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q3.9", "question": "What do pointer equality and integer round trips mean?", "options": "exposed-address equality with retained provenance / integers manufacture provenance / purely symbolic equality", "reason": "Equal addresses establish neither ownership nor interchangeable pointers. Integers carry no provenance, but from_exposed may recover the provenance of a live exposed block under the stated priority rule, including for fabricated integers. Borrow-derived exposure stays forbidden (OD64).", "rec": "`expose` records the block; `from_exposed` picks in-bounds, then one-past, else no provenance", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F5. expose(p) with(Unsafe) adds p's block to the exposed set (refused on borrow-derived pointers, OD64); releasing a block removes it. from_exposed(x): (1) the unique live exposed nonempty block containing x; else (2) the unique one with x = base+size; else no provenance. Zero-sized blocks are never candidates. Fabricated integer inside an exposed block → that block; reused address of a non-exposed block → none; a stale pointer keeps its old block and every access fails liveness.", "upd": true}
