{"id": "Q6.2", "question": "Is local constant propagation mandatory?", "options": "immutable scalar bindings only / none / general dataflow", "reason": "No branch/path inference, mutable-state propagation or user-function execution; perform this judgment independently of ANF temporaries so lowering cannot change acceptance.", "rec": "Fixed table of statically known values: literals, immutable lets, module constants, checked ops, six named wrapping/saturating ops, lossless `as`", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F12. Not known: mut locals, other calls, if/match results. Refused: a checked op that traps on known operands, division or remainder by a known zero (whatever the dividend), a shift by a known count < 0 or ≥ width, a fixed-array index known ≥ N. `as` never traps (only lossless casts exist, OD11). Evaluation runs no user function or loop; constants form an acyclic graph and are evaluated once, so there is no resource failure. Contract arithmetic is mathematical Int and is not part of this. The complete recognized call list is wrapping_add, wrapping_sub, wrapping_mul, saturating_add, saturating_sub, saturating_mul; each is known exactly when both operands are known, and adding a name is a spec change. A module constant is known iff its initializer is known. Initializer cycles are refused; each constant is evaluated once and memoized. MIN / -1 and MIN % -1 are refused when both operands are known.", "upd": true}
