{"id": "Q8.2", "question": "What exact source subset is admitted?", "options": "A1 minimal manifest / larger scalar language / informal examples", "reason": "Precisely admit the required `_` binding behavior and expected-type rules; unsupported constructs refuse explicitly. This gives both useful success and trap witnesses without silently expanding the slice.", "rec": "A1 minimal `u8`/linear-struct component subset", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q8.3", "question": "What establishes frontend correspondence?", "options": "independent byte-to-Core judgments / elaborator output defines correctness / parser tests alone", "reason": "Keep executable lexer/parser assurance separately identified rather than smuggling it into the word “source.”", "rec": "Independent byte-to-Core correspondence judgments", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q8.4", "question": "Does the first artifact qualify as a complete executable?", "options": "component plus bound launcher / extend slice to language `main` / call component a program", "reason": "This is an explicitly scoped component milestone; complete-language-executable qualification requires admitting and proving the real `main` path, without scalar parameters added to `main`.", "rec": "Component plus bound launcher first", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q8.5", "question": "What launcher ABI and observations are fixed?", "options": "exact profile contract / host defaults / stdout convention", "reason": "Observe returned value or terminal trap under a stated resource premise; launcher diagnostics and host failures are separately attributed.", "rec": "Pinned launcher ABI, inputs, startup and trap mapping", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q8.6", "question": "What artifacts does the claim bind?", "options": "complete dependency/image closure / source hash only / build label", "reason": "Bind identities through authenticated retained artifacts; a theorem about another build cannot qualify this image.", "rec": "Bind complete retained dependency and image closure", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q8.7", "question": "What prevents vacuous correctness?", "options": "independent coverage and environment/resource witnesses / successful-build refinement only", "reason": "A compiler that refuses everything or an empty admissible environment relation must fail qualification.", "rec": "Coverage, realizable witnesses and rejection controls; a spec where every allocation fails fails qualification", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F1 law 4: each target gives an explicit state and budget under which a nonempty test program succeeds. An always-failing allocator cannot qualify.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q9.1", "question": "What is Core’s expression and binding form?", "options": "ANF with places / nested expressions / SSA CFG", "reason": "References and views use dedicated scoped binders, not ordinary value `let`; initialization and replacement remain checked state transitions.", "rec": "ANF; fresh identities; explicit moves/copies; separate places", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F7: ANF also applies inside a loop's condition region, which is re-evaluated each iteration (Q9.3).", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q9.2", "question": "What types and declaration identities must Core represent?", "options": "complete kinded nominal inventory / slice-only types / backend layouts as types", "reason": "Keep nominal declaration, variant, trait and impl identities through substitution and linking; represent `Vec`/`String` through their owning nominal definitions rather than duplicate special Core types.", "rec": "Kinded nominal inventory; ordinary owning container definitions", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q9.3", "question": "What is the single loop form?", "options": "OD47 `while` / unconditional labelled `loop` / both", "reason": "OD47 already selects one Core `while` form for counted-loop elaboration; I see no demonstrated structural benefit requiring its revision. A labelled unconditional `loop` would need an explicit, justified OD47 amendment.", "rec": "Core `while L { C } do { B }`: C is a condition region re-run every iteration; the counted loop advances its counter before the body", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No; preserves OD47", "note": "R4 F7. C may call and have effects but discharges its own obligations and registers no defer. continue L / break L leave every inner scope, running deferred calls innermost first; continue then re-evaluates C. The counted loop: bounds are evaluated once; a hidden counter k advances before the body; the body sees i = the pre-advance value of k; so continue, which skips the rest of the body and re-tests k < hi, has already advanced i exactly once. Preserves OD47. Elaboration quoted from R4: '‘outer: for i from a until b { defer log_i(i); for j from 0 until n { defer tick(); if skip(i, j) { continue ‘outer; } use(i, j); } }' becomes '{ let lo = a; let hi = b; let mut k = lo; while Louter { k < hi } do { let i = k; k = k + 1; defer log_i(i); { let lo2 = 0; let hi2 = n; let mut k2 = lo2; while Linner { k2 < hi2 } do { let j = k2; k2 = k2 + 1; defer tick(); if skip(i, j) { continue Louter; } use(i, j); } } } }'. continue Louter runs tick(), leaves Linner, runs log_i(i), and re-tests k < hi. i and j are immutable; k and k2 cannot be named in source. The measure hi − k is proof-only, over mathematical integers. Effectful condition: 'while next(&mut r) != 0 { work(&mut r); }' becomes 'while L1 { let t1 = next(&mut r); let t2 = t1 != 0; t2 } do { work(&mut r); }', so next runs before every iteration.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q9.4", "question": "How are data, matching and exits represented?", "options": "flat constructors/destructures/matches and structured exits / source patterns / CFG only", "reason": "Elaborate nested patterns and conditionals once; keep checked operations intact until their lower-stage expansion.", "rec": "Flat data/matches; structured exits; checked stores", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F7: exits are resolved to loop identities (break L / continue L) with cleanup in reverse order.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q9.5", "question": "Are heap opening and borrowing separate user mechanisms?", "options": "one surface borrow with typed Core cases / two competing surface forms / implicit opening", "reason": "Each opens at most one layer, preserving root and lifetime; distinct semantic cases do not justify duplicate surface spellings.", "rec": "One surface borrow; distinct typed Core cases", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q9.6", "question": "Which operations are primitives rather than ordinary definitions?", "options": "irreducible operations plus checked library bodies / every builtin primitive / separate destructor engine", "reason": "`*h` and raw allocation/release need contracts; generic `destroy` resolves to its impl and executes that checked body. A specification table must not become a competing implementation of user destruction.", "rec": "Irreducible primitives; checked destructor bodies", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "Primitives only at irreducible boundaries (*h, raw allocation and release, I/O). destroy runs the type's checked impl body; no table re-implements user destruction. The impl inventory is Q2.2 (R4 F3).", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q9.7", "question": "How are primitive calls specified?", "options": "one typed contract-indexed inventory / parallel extern/intrinsic tables / strings with unchecked metadata", "reason": "Include generated operations, startup and shutdown; ordinary compound behavior comes from checked composition. Primitive identity must survive interfaces and specialization.", "rec": "One exhaustive typed primitive-contract inventory", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F5: the extern call-only/kept contract is its own row (Q3.12).", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q9.8", "question": "Does `defer` survive in Core?", "options": "nested registration scopes / immediate CFG expansion / eagerly captured arguments", "reason": "Run registered cleanups in reverse order on structured exits; retain place identities, late-read values, held loans and Unit-only calls. Evaluate return results into owned temporaries before cleanup; release each deferred loan after its call, skip cleanup on abort, and stop on a trapping cleanup.", "rec": "Nested deferred scopes; late reads; reverse cleanup", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q9.9", "question": "What is evaluator state and execution style?", "options": "total clock-indexed evaluator / partial interpreter / normative small-step plus separate runner", "reason": "Define maximal behaviors and adequacy; budget exhaustion is an interpreter result, not a program behavior.", "rec": "Total clock-indexed evaluator; explicit instrumented state", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q9.10", "question": "How are ownership instrumentation and erasure organized?", "options": "instrumented semantics plus proved erasure / ownership only in checker / runtime ownership tracking", "reason": "Distinguish uninitialized, moved and live obligations; erasure preserves observable behavior without deleting actual cleanup operations.", "rec": "Ghost ownership instrumentation with proved erasure", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q9.11", "question": "What do contracts mean and when are they checked?", "options": "erased logical contracts / implicit runtime assertions / optimizer assumptions without proof", "reason": "Postconditions describe normal returns after deferred cleanup, not trap freedom. requires reads parameters at entry; ensures reads result and surviving parameter state after cleanup. Consumed parameters and body locals are invisible. No snapshot construct is admitted; entry-to-exit relations are Lean theorems. Ghost code cannot consume runtime obligations or introduce effects.", "rec": "Erased logical contracts; ensures sees result and surviving parameters after cleanup; no snapshot construct", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F11. requires reads parameters at entry; ensures reads result and surviving parameter state after deferred calls (OD144). A ghost let is body-local and invisible to contracts, so it can't act as a snapshot. An entry-to-exit relation is a Lean theorem. Consistent with OD117.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q9.12", "question": "What evidence crosses declaration/module interfaces?", "options": "checked semantic interfaces plus reviewable rendering / signatures only / trust imported compiler summaries", "reason": "Human-readable Concrete declarations remain the review format; machine evidence is checked against those declarations and exact dependencies.", "rec": "Checked semantic interfaces with reviewable declarations", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q9.13", "question": "Are judgments independent of the checker and elaborator?", "options": "independent relations with soundness/correspondence / acceptance defines validity", "reason": "Prove executable checker and elaborator correspondence rather than blessing their output; supported-subset completeness is separate.", "rec": "Independent judgments; checker/elaborator correspondence", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q9.14", "question": "What must pass before Core freezes?", "options": "four checked representative proofs / prose inventory / full-language soundness first", "reason": "Check reset generativity/dependencies, deferred allocator/loan cleanup, heap opening/mutable lending and one values-to-bytes lemma; return substantive formalization discoveries to A2.", "rec": "Provisional approval, then four kernel-checked theorems against a proposed Spec.lean, then freeze", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F8. The four: reset_fresh (a reset gives a brand distinct from every live one), defer_cleanup_releases (structured exits run deferred destroy/give_up in reverse and release loans), open_mut_freezes_root (a mutable heap borrow blocks all other root access), rep_roundtrip (slice-1 types encode/decode). The sequence itself is row Q9.19. reset_fresh also proves that resets in a loop give pairwise-distinct brands and that old-brand values are refused by the new allocator's operations. defer_cleanup_releases covers every structured exit, releases each held loan after its call, and preserves the allocator-dependency check. open_mut_freezes_root also proves a shared open never grants writes. rep_roundtrip covers u8–u64, bool and the empty struct.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q9.15", "question": "Where do normative definitions and documentation live?", "options": "one main Lean file with doc comments / many normative modules / generated prose specification", "reason": "Keep syntax, independent judgments, semantics, elaboration and primitive contracts together in ordered namespaces; supporting proof/test files import it and do not redefine rules. This is your fixed decision, superseding snapshot generated-chapter requirements.", "rec": "One main normative Lean file with doc comments", "agree": "Y", "revises": "Generated-chapter requirement (philosophy rule 6 / earlier roadmap commitment): replaced by one normative Lean file with doc comments and examples, a browsable rendering of that Lean, and coverage-gated retirement of prose chapters; your decision of 2026-11-04", "note": "Your decision of 2026-11-04. R4 F8: the file starts as a PROPOSED draft during A2b and becomes normative at freeze.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q9.16", "question": "What Lean dependencies may the specification use?", "options": "core Lean only / core plus pinned Batteries / Mathlib throughout", "reason": "Dependency placement, not library reputation, should keep the executable semantic foundation small; imported proof dependencies remain in the audited closure.", "rec": "Core Lean; supporting Batteries; owner exceptions for Mathlib", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "Agreed after Claude's pushback: Spec.lean imports only core Lean; supporting proofs may use pinned Batteries; Mathlib only by a recorded owner exception per proof area. No current proof needs Mathlib."}
{"id": "Q9.17", "question": "May spec proofs use `native_decide`?", "options": "kernel-only authoritative proofs / labelled native-evaluation proofs / unrestricted use", "reason": "Use `decide` or proof-producing tactics whose terms pass the declared axiom policy; native evaluation may accelerate tests, but any separately retained native-axiom evidence must carry its distinct trust label.", "rec": "Kernel-only authoritative spec/A2 proofs", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q9.18", "question": "How is the old compiler retained?", "options": "isolated `legacy/` / delete after slice 1 / mix old and new modules", "reason": "This implements your newer fixed decision while preserving replay and the differential oracle; legacy behavior cannot become a normative fallback.", "rec": "Frozen isolated `legacy/`; no normative fallback", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "Your decision of 2026-11-04. The move plan is being simplified (see Other decisions)."}
{"id": "Q9.19", "question": "How do approval, formalization and Core freeze relate?", "options": "A2a provisional approval → A2b proposed Spec.lean with four kernel-checked theorems → A2c freeze / approve and freeze at once", "reason": "The four proofs are required before freeze but need a spec to be stated against. Provisional approval makes the rows the design to formalize; anything formalization changes goes back to you before freeze.", "rec": "A2a → A2b → A2c; Spec.lean headed PROPOSED until freeze; R-0560 depends on the freeze", "agree": "Y", "revises": "Process: owner decision", "note": "R4 F8. A2a approval binds the design without freezing it. A2b uses ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean headed 'PROPOSED: not normative until Core freeze'; all four theorems are kernel-checked without native_decide. Changed rows return to you. A2c needs your approval, the four checked theorems and an outside-reader pass; only then is PROPOSED removed and A3's one-main-file gate applied.", "upd": true, "new": true}

