 succeeded in 0ms:
{"id": "Q1.7", "question": "Where does release information live?", "options": "stable state plus raw context / relocatable state / safe allocator reference in each allocation", "reason": "The dependency proof validates the context, including release-code identity and state lifetime; allocations never store safe references.", "rec": "Stable release state and raw context; no safe references", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q3.1", "question": "What does opening a heap array produce?", "options": "second-class shared/mutable views / element references / first-class slices", "reason": "Keep `len`, Copy `get`/`set`, and general mutable `replace`/`swap`; bounds failures trap and no operation returns an element reference.", "rec": "Second-class shared/mutable array views; no element references", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q3.2", "question": "How are views passed and lent?", "options": "bare downward parameters with implicit mutable lending / first-class moves / no passing", "reason": "Mutable lending freezes the originating access until return; views cannot be returned, captured, aggregated or introduced through ordinary `let`.", "rec": "Downward parameters; mutable lending freezes origin", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q3.3", "question": "What is a view’s root identity?", "options": "original owner binding / view-local identity / physical address", "reason": "Opening through a shell reference retains its root and lifetime; opening never upgrades shared access.", "rec": "Original owner identity plus projection path", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q3.4", "question": "Which view accesses conflict?", "options": "whole-root conflicts / disjoint-index borrowing / runtime alias checks", "reason": "Two disjoint indices do not create an exception to REF-11; a mutable view conflicts with other access to its owner root.", "rec": "Existing whole-root conflict rules", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q3.5", "question": "What is the index type?", "options": "`u64` / new `usize` / any integer", "reason": "One length and index type, u64, on every target, with explicit conversions; each target states a checked maximum allocation size, which is part of Avail.", "rec": "`u64` lengths/indices across profiles; checked target limits", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No; strengthens cross-profile policy", "note": "Agreed after Claude's pushback: u64 lengths and indices on every target, and each target states a checked maximum allocation size. The meaning of a program never depends on the target.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q3.6", "question": "How are nested linear elements traversed without substitutes?", "options": "consuming traversal / temporary invalid slot / element reference", "reason": "A transformation consumes each element and returns its replacement or recovery obligations; no safe operation exposes a hole or adds forbidden element references.", "rec": "`drain` / `map_into` for consuming traversal; `swap_at(v, i, x) -> T` for in-place replacement", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F2. No element references. swap_at traps on i ≥ len, like indexing.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q3.7", "question": "What is the raw-memory model and validity taxonomy?", "options": "block–offset with provenance / concrete addresses alone / untyped bytes alone", "reason": "State initialization, bounds, alignment, lifetime, provenance, exclusivity where required, transfer, frame and foreign mutation; unsafe authorization proves none of these premises.", "rec": "Blocks with provenance; pointer = (prov: Option<BlockId>, addr: u64); integers carry no provenance; every access needs explicit validity premises", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F5. An access needs provenance, bounds, liveness, alignment, initialization (read) and write access (write). A violated premise is a failed proof obligation of the Unsafe code, never defined behaviour. Zero-byte accesses need only alignment and a non-null address.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q3.8", "question": "How do zero-sized elements behave?", "options": "logical slots with independent ownership / forbid them / identify slots by address", "reason": "Length and element obligations remain real even when byte stride is zero; equal addresses never justify duplication, overlapping ownership or skipped destruction.", "rec": "Zero-sized slots own their obligations; they occupy no bytes and may share addresses", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F13. Each zero-sized element still has its own obligation and is destroyed in index order. Ownership and identity never come from address equality.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q3.9", "question": "What do pointer equality and integer round trips mean?", "options": "exposed-address equality with retained provenance / integers manufacture provenance / purely symbolic equality", "reason": "Equal addresses establish neither ownership nor interchangeable pointers. Integers carry no provenance, but from_exposed may recover the provenance of a live exposed block under the stated priority rule, including for fabricated integers. Borrow-derived exposure stays forbidden (OD64).", "rec": "`expose` records the block; `from_exposed` picks in-bounds, then one-past, else no provenance", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F5. expose(p) with(Unsafe) adds p's block to the exposed set (refused on borrow-derived pointers, OD64); releasing a block removes it. from_exposed(x): (1) the unique live exposed nonempty block containing x; else (2) the unique one with x = base+size; else no provenance. Zero-sized blocks are never candidates. Fabricated integer inside an exposed block → that block; reused address of a non-exposed block → none; a stale pointer keeps its old block and every access fails liveness.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q3.10", "question": "What happens when address space runs out?", "options": "checked operation failure / wraparound / unrestricted abstract supply", "reason": "Addresses never wrap or collide between live regions; other runtime exhaustion stays separately accounted for.", "rec": "A collision means overlapping occupied bytes; address exhaustion is part of Avail", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No", "note": "R4 F13. Every block has base+size ≤ 2^64−1, so one-past is representable. An allocation that does not fit is an ordinary AllocFailure. ptr_eq compares addresses only and grants nothing.", "upd": true}
{"id": "Q3.11", "question": "How are typed values related to raw bytes?", "options": "structured values plus raw store and `Rep` / all values are bytes / unrelated models", "reason": "It covers layout, initialization, pointer fragments, permissions and external frames; load/store and ownership-transfer correspondence require proofs.", "rec": "Structured values and raw store joined by `Rep`", "agree": "Y", "revises": "No"}
{"id": "Q3.12", "question": "How are extern pointer parameters marked, and how does C keep memory past the call?", "options": "(a) parameter marker `kept buf: H` (no marker = call-only) / (b) attribute `#[kept]` / (c) markers on both forms", "reason": "OD64 fixes call-only as the default and kept as the exception; the receipt-based ownership design is a new owner decision. A kept parameter takes an owned allocation by value and returns a linear receipt Kept<H> that only its paired extern can turn back into H. The default is the safe case and the exception is visible at the declaration.", "rec": "(a) `kept` parameter marker; `Kept<H>` receipt ended only by the paired extern", "agree": "Y", "revises": "New design + spelling: owner decision", "note": "R4 F5.4. extern fn ring_submit(kept buf: HeapArray<u8, Sys>) -> Kept<HeapArray<u8, Sys>>; extern fn ring_complete(r: Kept<HeapArray<u8, Sys>>) -> HeapArray<u8, Sys>. Kept<H> is linear, has no operations, is not Destroy, and keeps its allocator alive. That C stops using the memory before the paired call returns is a listed trusted-base assumption. Borrow-derived pointers can never reach a kept parameter. A call-only parameter permits reads, and writes only when the argument came from a &mut or an owned allocation, during the call only; no access after return is a named trusted-base assumption. The submitting extern's contract names its paired completion extern, and the checker verifies the pair takes exactly Kept<H> and returns H. C receives the ABI pointer and length.", "upd": true, "new": true}
{"id": "Q7.10", "question": "Do you approve the linux-x86_64-hosted grant table?", "options": "Approve as written / change specific rows", "reason": "One concrete table instead of 'where supported' categories: every grant's instances, launch flag, operations with their permissions, narrowing, and how it ends.", "rec": "Approve R4 F6's complete grant table below as part of this row: every launch configuration, operation signature, permission, narrowing rule and ending contract. Standard-stream operations require Console (Q7.11), not Device; where the R4 F6 table says with(Device) for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr, read with(Console). A missing configured grant refuses startup with exit 125; a trap exits 134.", "agree": "Y", "revises": "New target contract: owner decision", "note": "R4 F6 table. Allocator<Sys> (≤1): alloc, arenas, with(Alloc); arenas made from it own their backing (Q1.6); ends by give_up(a) with(Alloc) -> Unit, only with no live allocation. Directory (one per name, --dir NAME=PATH:RIGHTS, rights ⊆ {list, read, write, create, remove}, with(Fs)): narrow(d, r) with(Fs) -> Directory consumes, rights ∩ r, cannot fail; open_dir(&d, path, r) with(Fs) -> Result<Directory, FsError> gives a child with rights ⊆ parent ∩ r and d stays; ends by close_dir(d) with(Fs) -> Unit, consuming in every outcome. File (acquired, not granted; rights per open mode): close(f) with(Fs) -> Result<Unit, CloseError>, consuming in every outcome; CloseError is Copy and holds no handle. Environment (≤1, --arg …, --env NAME allowlist): args and allowlisted vars read with(Env) through scoped views, no allocation; narrow_env(e, names) with(Env) -> Environment consumes; give_up(e) with(Env) -> Unit. Clock<Monotonic>, Clock<Wall> (≤1 each): now(&c) with(Time) -> u64 ns; resolution from clock_getres, recorded in the launch manifest; Wall may go backward; give_up(c) with(Time) -> Unit. StdIn, StdOut, StdErr (≤1 each): read or write bytes with(Console); give_up(s) with(Console) -> Result<Unit, IoError> flushes, consumes in every outcome, and leaves the descriptor open for runtime diagnostics. ConnectGrant (one per name, --connect NAME=tcp:CIDR,…:PORTS): connect(&g, addr) with(Network) -> Result<Socket, NetError>, g stays; narrow_connect(g, scope) with(Network) -> ConnectGrant consumes, scope ∩; give_up(g) with(Network) -> Unit. ListenGrant (one per name, --listen NAME=tcp:ADDR:PORT): listen(g) with(Network) -> Result<Listener, ListenFailed { grant: ListenGrant, err: NetError }>; ends through the listener's close(l) with(Network), or give_up(g) with(Network) -> Unit. Resolver (one per name, --resolve NAME=server:IP:PORT;transport:udp|tcp;suffix:S,…;types:A,AAAA): resolve(&r, name, a) with(Network, Alloc) -> Result<Vec<IpAddr, A>, ResolveError>; a name or type outside the scope fails before any query; addresses grant nothing; give_up(r) with(Network) -> Unit. Socket and Listener close like File: close(s) with(Network) -> Result<Unit, CloseError>, always consuming. Trace events: Start(grants), Exit(code: u8), Trap(kind): the runtime writes one line naming the trap and its source location to descriptor 2 (OD101's content) and exits 134. No process spawning. A parameter type this target does not offer is a compile error.", "upd": true, "new": true}

